 scan: {title:'Rainfall is becoming harder to plan around', body:'In the fictional coastal region of Luma, seasonal rainfall is becoming less reliable. The worked example asks what this could mean for preparedness over the next six months.', points:[['Trend','Increasing variability in the onset and distribution of seasonal rainfall.'],['Direction','Difficult to assess from a short record.'],['Illustrative evidence','An invented station summary dated 1 October reports more uneven rainfall in recent seasons. A fictional responder discussion dated 4 October describes planting disruption. Neither is a real source.'],['Uncertainty','The short station record may not represent the whole region; local accounts vary.'],['Outlook','Continued irregularity could disrupt planting and household income. The next season’s rainfall and access to alternative livelihoods could change this trajectory.']]},
 prioritise: {title:'Judge a defined proposition', body:'Assess this proposition: “Over the next six months, continued rainfall irregularity will disrupt planting and increase pressure on household food access in Luma.”', points:[['Illustrative placement','Medium likelihood, high impact — discussion labels, not a forecast or calculated score.'],['Reasoning','The imagined evidence suggests disruption is plausible, but geographic coverage and household coping capacity are unclear.'],['Other candidates','Rising dependence on imported staples; changes in access to seasonal work. These are fictional comparison trends.'],['Watch list','An unusually severe coastal flood is retained for discussion despite a low likelihood judgement.'],['Group decision','A vote draws attention to rainfall variability, but the group still checks overlap, evidence gaps and missing perspectives.']]},
 narrative: {title:'Connect change to consequences', body:'The group combines overlapping food-access trends and focuses on the mechanisms that connect rainfall variability to possible needs.', points:[['Driver','Increasing seasonal rainfall variability.'],['Trend','More frequent disruption to planting and agricultural income.'],['Possible consequence','Pressure on food access for households with limited savings or alternatives.'],['Caveat','Market access, social support, crop choices and local response capacity could interrupt this pathway.'],['Overarching story','Environmental variability and livelihood constraints may interact. The briefing explores where that interaction could strain household capacity, rather than listing each change separately.']]},
 write: {title:'Draft an argument people can examine', body:'A fictional briefing excerpt: “Uneven seasonal rainfall could increase pressure on agricultural livelihoods in Luma over the next six months. The extent of any food-access consequences would depend on market conditions, household coping options and support.”', points:[['Actors','Farming households, local traders, agricultural advisers, community groups and local responders.'],['Evidence','The invented station summary and fictional responder discussion from the scan; both require scrutiny within the example.'],['Alternative interpretation','The apparent shift could reflect short-term variability rather than an enduring pattern.'],['Indicators','Rainfall distribution, planting progress, staple prices and reported coping strategies.'],['Audience consideration','Preparedness teams could examine which assumptions in their existing plans depend on a predictable planting season. This is a question for exploration, not an operational recommendation.']]},
 review: {title:'Let the challenge change the draft', body:'A reviewer notices that the briefing implies a region-wide pattern from a limited station record. The group narrows the claim and makes the geographic uncertainty explicit.', points:[['Challenge','Could different rainfall patterns or household resources produce different consequences within Luma?'],['Revision','Replace a region-wide claim with a conditional account for the areas represented in the invented evidence.'],['Remaining gap','Perspectives from households outside the station coverage are missing.'],['Follow-through','Record what observations would change the assessment and who would review them.'],['Lesson','A more qualified conclusion can be more useful than a more dramatic one. All details in this worked example remain fictional.']]}
